{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And the name of the Israelite man.</b> It is difficult: whichever way you take it. If HaShem wished to reveal those who were struck, He should have mentioned them at the time of the deed, when He said (Numbers 25:6): ״וְהִנֵּה אִישׁ מִבְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — there was the place to mention him and say ״וְהִנֵּה אִישׁ וְגוֹ׳ זִמְרִי״ and so on, and when He mentioned the Midianite woman as well, He should have mentioned her name. And if the Torah covered them over in the manner that it covered over the wood-gatherer on Shabbat, why did He reconsider to mention their names, and it was also necessary to add still further superfluous words in the Torah — for if He had mentioned their names above, there would have been no need to say a second time ״וְשֵׁם אִישׁ וְגוֹ׳ וְשֵׁם הָאִשָּׁה״ and so on?" "<b>Indeed,</b> behold, the Master blessed be He does not wish to degrade even the wicked by publicizing who the detested perpetrators are, and the wood-gatherer proves this. Also in the matter before us you will see that He did not reveal them at the time of the deed, but only after He mentioned the praise of Phinehas, who acted and performed the good deed of being zealous for HaShem and making atonement for the children of Israel — only then did He also recall that he was zealous not against an ordinary man but against a great man, a prince of a father's house, together with the woman who was head of the kelipot and whose father was a king, as He said: ״רֹאשׁ אֻמּוֹת בֵּית אָב״, and she was abominated by a degrading death before the eyes of all; and in everything of this kind His name blessed be He is sanctified. Therefore He ascribed her as being the daughter of a king. And although there is degradation for the Israelite man, it does not diminish the eye of the righteous, as Scripture says (Proverbs 10:7): ״זֵכֶר צַדִּיק לִבְרָכָה״ — even though ״שֵׁם רְשָׁעִים יִרְקָב״ in the midst of it. Or perhaps the verse comes to inform you that he did not achieve the turning back of wrath and so on except through the mediation of this deed that the zealotry against a prince accomplished, and His name was sanctified with a great sanctification, and the power of the Other Side [ס״מ] was subdued, and the sin became so despised in everyone's eyes — through this he turned back wrath and made atonement and so on; but without this, even had he been zealous for HaShem against an ordinary man, he would not have achieved all the gradations that are inscribed." "And examine closely that it says <b>the name of the man</b> and was not satisfied with saying ״the name of the struck one״ and so on. According to our way, it intends the language of prominence, to say that he was distinguished and eminent in Israel, and despite all this this righteous man did not restrain himself from doing through him the wondrous deed. It further appears that the reason HaShem did not mention his name above (Numbers 25:6) is because he had not yet committed the deed but only intended to do it; and as long as he had not acted, the Torah would not disgrace him by mentioning his name. But after he had already committed the deed, HaShem publicized his name, for it is a mitzvah to publicize the wicked." "<b>The one who was struck, who was struck.</b> One needs to know why it repeated to say <b>the one who was struck, who was struck</b> and was not satisfied with one of them. Also one needs to know why Scripture changed the natural manner of expression when it began to speak of him at the mention of the Israelite's name — there it first said ״an Israelite man״ and afterward said the event, ״the one who was struck״ and so on, whereas when it mentioned the Midianite woman it first gave the event and the name of the woman who was struck and only afterward said ״the Midianite״?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the matter may be explained according to their words, of blessed memory (Berakhot 18b), who said that the wicked in their lifetime are called dead, and the reason is that the evil force, which is the aspect of death, is attached to them. And I have already written in several places that the name by which a person is called is the name of the soul, and HaShem places names in the earth, as they said of blessed memory (Berakhot 7a): do not read שְׁמוֹת (names) but שֵׁמוֹת, and so on. When a person sins, his soul is blemished by the attachment of evil, the aspect of the rotting, and this is what Scripture says (Proverbs 10:7): ״וְשֵׁם רְשָׁעִים יִרְקָב״, and therefore Rabbi Meir would examine the name (Yoma 83b) to see whether he was a possessor of a straight soul. Now this one who consorted with an Aramaean woman corrupted his soul by encountering her, and behold he is struck with the blow of death before Phinehas killed him. This is what the verse intends to say: <b>the name of the Israelite man, the one who was struck, who was struck</b> and so on — corresponding to the blow of the soul it says ״the name of the Israelite man, the one who was struck,״ for from the time he loathed his soul and came upon her, he was struck with a blow to the Israelite soul, which is the designation of the holy soul called Israel. And corresponding to his being killed — which Phinehas killed him — it says ״who was struck אֶת״ and so on, as Scripture says (Numbers 25:8): ״וַיִּדְקֹר אֶת שְׁנֵיהֶם״. Or on this path: ״the one who was struck״ means that Phinehas struck him; ״who was struck אֶת the Midianite״ — that is the blow of the soul that he implanted through the word אִישׁ. And it is precisely parsed also what it says: ״הֻכָּה אֶת הַמִּדְיָנִית,״ for because he lay with her, he was struck in his soul; and accordingly the parsing of the word אֲשֶׁר הֻכָּה means: he had already been struck, namely the Midianite — meaning, through his being with her." "<b>Further</b>, he hinted by saying <b>et the Midianite woman</b> in the manner of what Scripture says (Genesis 39:10): ״וְלֹא שָׁמַע אֵלֶיהָ לִשְׁכַּב אֶצְלָהּ לִהְיוֹת עִמָּהּ״, and the Sages of blessed memory said (Yoma 35a): ״לִשְׁכַּב אֶצְלָהּ״ — in this world; ״לִהְיוֹת עִמָּהּ״ — for the World to Come. And this is the meaning of <b>he was struck et the Midianite woman</b> — that the striking is that he would be אִתָּהּ עִמָּהּ, with her in the World to Come, for he presumably died without repentance, since he was pierced in the moment of the act. And through this is resolved what we examined: why it doubled ״הַמֻּכֶּה אֲשֶׁר הֻכָּה״, and also the reason that it preceded by saying ״אִישׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ before saying ״הַמֻּכֶּה״ — in order that the phrase ״הַמֻּכֶּה״ would refer back to the name, to say that the striking came upon him also under the name ״אִישׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל״, which is a branch of holiness. And therefore when it mentions the Midianite woman it mentions in her case only one striking, as it is written ״וְשֵׁם הָאִשָּׁה הַמֻּכָּה״; also it did not precede her mention before the mention of the striking, for in her lifetime she is called dead, and her name remains מֻכֶּה, standing as a mark within the word ״מִדְיָנִית״. And we have already written in several places that the name of idolaters and constellations is the name of the klipot, all of which are aspects of death." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained along this line based on what has come before us from the words of the mekubbalim: that none who is cast off shall be cast off from all the sparks of holiness, and all of them in the end will merit to return to the place from which they came. And even if a man of Israel acts wickedly and even destroys his soul, in the end he will return to his root. And this is the meaning of <b>and the name of the man of Israel</b> — see that even after the act he is called by the name Israel; from this you learn that he was not uprooted from his root. <b>הַמֻּכֶּה</b> — meaning that Pinhas struck him; the verse informs you that he was not struck with the loss of his soul, but rather ״אֲשֶׁר הֻכָּה אֶת הַמִּדְיָנִית״ — meaning, that evil part that had cleaved to him from the Midianite woman, such that the name of striking would apply to it — that is what was struck and died through the striking of Pinhas. From this you learn that his loss — what he lost through the transgression — was exacted from him; the sin was collected from him through the striking of Pinhas, who killed him, and his death was his atonement. Or along this line: that he was not struck in his soul except while he was with her, but after he was separated from her no trace of the sin remained in him, for he was separated through death and his death purified his soul. And through this a reason is given for why he was called a man of Israel, for after the killing he is called by the name Israel. This is what I have written, if it is possible."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {4 "The text ״אֲשֶׁר הֻכָּה אֶת הַמִּדְיָנִית״ (Numbers 25:14) is rendered hyper-literally throughout, preserving the anomalous אֶת particle and the passive construction; the final parsing reads הֻכָּה as though its subject is the Israelite whose soul-wound was caused by his encounter with the Midianite woman."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:56.192307232Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}